WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.945
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.945 --> 00:00:02.566
Friday the twenty-third.

00:00:02.566 --> 00:00:07.359
Half day at work, then a long, social stretch that runs into the night.

00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:09.790
Something to chew on while you move.

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:13.301
How long does it take Phobos to go once around Mars?

00:00:13.301 --> 00:00:14.651
Answer's at the end.

00:00:14.651 --> 00:00:15.664
Weather's easy.

00:00:15.664 --> 00:00:21.403
Sunny and dry, high around sixty, with a light north wind that won't bother the bike.

00:00:21.403 --> 00:00:26.940
It drops into the low forties tonight, so the ride home after drinks will be cold.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:27.885
Okay, the day.

00:00:27.885 --> 00:00:33.962
Gym at seven, straight sets again, so save the myo-reps for the raises and the rear delts.

00:00:33.962 --> 00:00:37.203
Then Godinger, but only a half day, nine to one.

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:40.646
Lunch is at one-thirty, close to work in Ridgewood.

00:00:40.646 --> 00:00:44.630
Somewhere in the afternoon there's the lens rental handoff.

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:50.976
The time still isn't locked, and lunch runs till two-thirty, so try to pin it for right after.

00:00:50.976 --> 00:00:54.892
Clean and test the lens first, and check the renter's I.D.

00:00:54.892 --> 00:01:00.631
At five it's climbing, then dinner and drinks with friends, probably till around ten.

00:01:00.631 --> 00:01:01.577
Alright, news.

00:01:01.577 --> 00:01:11.029
The big tech story this week is a pair of serious holes in PHP, the language that quietly runs a huge chunk of the web, including WordPress.

00:01:11.029 --> 00:01:17.038
Under the right conditions, an attacker could take over a server without ever logging in.

00:01:17.038 --> 00:01:20.077
Researchers found them with help from an A.I.

00:01:20.077 --> 00:01:23.655
model, after they'd sat there for more than a decade.

00:01:23.655 --> 00:01:31.622
When they went public Tuesday, Finland pulled its government websites offline and even parts of Wikipedia blinked out.

00:01:31.622 --> 00:01:37.159
A patch landed Wednesday afternoon, and security firms are already seeing attacks.

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:43.911
If you've got a portfolio site on WordPress, or a host that runs it for you, make sure it's updated.

00:01:43.911 --> 00:01:45.869
Markets had a rough Thursday.

00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:53.566
The Nasdaq fell more than two and a half percent, its worst day since July, and the S&P dropped about one percent.

00:01:53.566 --> 00:01:56.266
Investors are nervous that building A.I.

00:01:56.266 --> 00:02:02.005
data centers on borrowed money has gotten expensive, and that the stocks ran too far.

00:02:02.005 --> 00:02:12.808
At the same time, oil jumped to its highest price in about five months, because nobody knows when the war with Iran will let energy shipping get back to normal.

00:02:12.808 --> 00:02:17.670
And one small one, since you picked this show's voice off a leaderboard.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:25.434
HeyGen has a new voice model called Voice Turbo that just took the top spot in that same Artificial Analysis arena.

00:02:25.434 --> 00:02:30.701
Knowing how tight those top spots are, call it worth a listen, not a new king.

00:02:30.701 --> 00:02:38.803
For the weekend, there's a cocktail bar on Willoughby Avenue called The Museum Bar, built like a natural history museum.

00:02:38.803 --> 00:02:52.644
You come in through a tiny gift shop bar with lighter drinks, walk through a hall of crystals and fossils, and end up in the main room, where the menu is split into permanent exhibits and special exhibits.

00:02:52.644 --> 00:02:56.358
It opened this summer, so it might've slipped past you.

00:02:56.358 --> 00:03:02.097
It's open Sunday from five, which makes it an easy, low-key night after a big Friday.

00:03:02.097 --> 00:03:04.325
Today's challenge is a photo one.

00:03:04.325 --> 00:03:10.469
At dinner tonight, take one picture of the table using only the light that's already there.

00:03:10.469 --> 00:03:11.077
No flash.

00:03:11.077 --> 00:03:14.115
A candle, a pendant lamp, whatever's closest.

00:03:14.115 --> 00:03:20.124
Shift your angle until the light comes across faces from the side instead of straight on.

00:03:20.124 --> 00:03:22.082
One frame you like is enough.

00:03:22.082 --> 00:03:24.580
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:28.226
Remember when Gemini's voice only handed back raw PCM?

00:03:28.226 --> 00:03:29.779
Here's what that means.

00:03:29.779 --> 00:03:32.682
PCM is sound written down as plain numbers.

00:03:32.682 --> 00:03:38.827
A microphone's signal gets measured over and over, and each measurement becomes one number.

00:03:38.827 --> 00:03:46.321
For the memo's pipeline, that's twenty-four thousand measurements every second, each one sixteen bits, in mono.

00:03:46.321 --> 00:03:48.954
The catch is that raw PCM has no label.

00:03:48.954 --> 00:03:55.774
It's just a long stream of numbers, with nothing saying how fast to play them or how big each one is.

00:03:55.774 --> 00:04:02.323
That's why the render script has to spell it out, sixteen bit, twenty-four thousand, one channel.

00:04:02.323 --> 00:04:08.197
A WAV file is basically the same PCM with a tiny header on top that carries that label.

00:04:08.197 --> 00:04:09.818
MP3 is a different idea.

00:04:09.818 --> 00:04:11.573
It doesn't store the wave.

00:04:11.573 --> 00:04:15.016
It stores a recipe for something that sounds close.

00:04:15.016 --> 00:04:20.823
It breaks the sound into frequencies, then leans on a trick of hearing called masking.

00:04:20.823 --> 00:04:26.900
A loud sound hides quieter sounds near it in pitch, so the encoder just throws those away.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:28.182
The payoff is size.

00:04:28.182 --> 00:04:32.504
Raw, the memo runs about three hundred eighty kilobits a second.

00:04:32.504 --> 00:04:34.597
The MP3 goes out at ninety-six.

00:04:34.597 --> 00:04:42.159
So a five minute episode shrinks from around fourteen megabytes to under four and you can't hear the difference.

00:04:42.159 --> 00:04:48.303
So: gym at seven, work till one, lunch at one-thirty, lens handoff after, climbing at five.

00:04:48.303 --> 00:04:54.312
And the answer: Phobos laps Mars in about seven and a half hours, faster than Mars spins.

00:04:54.312 --> 00:04:56.000
Have a great climb, Ziad.
